Z||000017||Philphine||01-30-2001||01:10 PM||||i think tdc was gm's attempt at coming up wth a name for their dohc engines. but someone in marketing didn't talk to the engineers to find out it already had a meaning.<P>what i wonder and it's not just for fieros, what is ICE? i know it refers to car stereo, but dose it stand for something?  ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Standard||01-30-2001||01:55 PM||||ICE usually means Internal Combustion Engine.  Haven't heard it used for anything stereo related, unless it's for some manufacturer's buzzwords..||||reg||
Z||000019||Oreif||01-30-2001||02:31 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>oreif no one cam per head=sohc can be one in each head in v6 0r v8 like new ford.<BR>dohc= 2per head not motor total can be dohc-v6 with 4 cams, still dohc if only 2 valve and some 4 valve with only one cam rare .<BR>allso some with 3valve honda and 5valve bikes and race motors. ray </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>HUH?<BR>There is not a DOHC engine (for cars) that has 2 valves per cylinder and 2 cams in the head. There is no such thing as a 4-valve head with single cam for cars. In the V-6 and V-8 engines with a single cam in each head, depending on the company, they are referred to as dual cam or twin cam engines. I am not sure about new ford engines but many manufacturers stay away from single overhead cams on "V" type engines for reliability reasons. This is a car forum not a bike/motorcycle forum.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-30-2001).]||||reg||1
